Subject: [PATCH] sender: gate the protocol>27 extra end-of-phase marker on the
 negotiated version, not the peer's announced version

When openrsync is the server-sender and the remote is a newer rsync, the
peer announces a protocol version > 27 (e.g. 31).  openrsync only speaks
protocol 27, so the effective/negotiated version is min(lver, rver) = 27.

send_up_fsm() emitted a second -1 end-of-phase marker whenever the peer's
*announced* version (sess->rver) was > 27.  But that extra marker is a
protocol-30+ behaviour and must be keyed off the *negotiated* version.
Emitting it while actually speaking protocol 27 desynchronises the
stream and hangs the peer (e.g. a stock rsync client pulling from an
openrsync server).

Since openrsync hardcodes lver = RSYNC_PROTOCOL (27), MINIMUM(lver, rver)
is always <= 27 and the extra marker is never (incorrectly) sent.

diff --git a/usr.bin/rsync/sender.c b/usr.bin/rsync/sender.c
index a5e4f3f..3d87886 100644
--- a/usr.bin/rsync/sender.c
+++ b/usr.bin/rsync/sender.c
@@ -224,7 +224,7 @@ send_up_fsm(struct sess *sess, size_t *phase,
 		}
 		io_lowbuffer_int(sess, *wb, &pos, *wbsz, -1);
 
-		if (sess->opts->server && sess->rver > 27) {
+		if (sess->opts->server && MINIMUM(sess->lver, sess->rver) > 27) {
 			if (!io_lowbuffer_alloc(sess,
 			    wb, wbsz, wbmax, isz)) {
 				ERRX1("io_lowbuffer_alloc");
